  {
    title: 'AI Diplomacy — Theory of Mind as Engineering',
    description:
      'Seven LLM powers negotiate, ally and betray each other in full-rules Diplomacy, each keeping an explicit trust model of the others that updates separately on what they promise and on what they then order. The question was whether belief modelling can be built as engineering rather than prompting, measured so that the answer could be no - and on this run it was: a matched-seed ablation scored evaluator AUC 0.497, which is chance. Live replay viewer on GitHub Pages.',
    problem:
      'Multi-agent LLM demos are easy to make look impressive and nearly impossible to evaluate. An agent that says it "trusts France at 0.7" is producing a number, not a belief - and nothing in a transcript distinguishes a calibrated model from a confident one. Worse, an agent that can see its counterpart\'s private channel produces plausible results that are quietly meaningless.',
    approach:
      'Trust is a Beta posterior over promise-keeping, not an LLM-supplied score: every message carries a structured commitment, and after adjudication the engine diffs promised against ordered, which is the evidence the posterior updates on. Betrayals weigh double, because alliances take many turns to build and one stab to destroy. Two properties are enforced mechanically rather than by instruction - a belief store belongs to one power and has no method that could admit another\'s private channel, and a structurally separate evaluator scores incoming messages while seeing only the counterpart\'s words and public order record, never its own side\'s plans. Both are asserted by fixtures that must fail.',
    impact:
      'The definitive ablation (12 seeds x 2 arms, all seven powers LLM-driven on claude-haiku-4.5, about $25) scored evaluator AUC 0.497, 95% CI [0.453, 0.542], on 667 falsifiable commitments: kept promises do not rank above broken ones, and the low-confidence predictions run backwards (it said 15%, the promise was kept 80% of the time). The Theory of Mind claim is not supported by this run. Getting to a negative worth trusting took three rounds of fixing the measurement itself: a cross-process nondeterminism bug that would have had the two arms playing different games (now a CI job), silent fallbacks that made 47% of one batch\'s "evaluator scores" a constant, and a "kept" base rate of 82% that turned out to be mostly unfalsifiable promises. What remains untested - a stronger evaluator model, richer board context, longer games - is named rather than implied to be an obvious fix.',
