    title: 'Convex Optimization App — Live Optimization Workbench',
    description:
      'Three classic convex optimization problems solved by four first-order methods written from scratch in NumPy, benchmarked against SciPy ground truth, and served as a public web workbench with an LLM natural-language front door. Deployed live on fly.io with a same-origin React UI.',
    problem:
      'Most optimization education stops at library calls - you never see the method, the convergence behavior, or what happens when it fails. And an LLM "solve my problem" front door usually means silently trusting the LLM.',
    approach:
      'Implemented gradient descent, Nesterov acceleration, ISTA and FISTA from scratch with per-iterate convergence history; nothing is tuned (fixed 1/L step, tol 1e-10, 2000-iteration cap), so the same problem+seed returns the byte-identical response. SciPy appears only as an independent referee. POST /parse turns plain English into a structured solve request, which a deterministic mechanical verifier checks against the literal text before anything runs; mismatches are surfaced, never hidden. One fly.io process serves the API and the React workbench same-origin, with bounded rate limiting, body caps, and honest failure states (a "hit the iteration cap" result is shown as an outcome, not an error).',
    impact:
